\u00b2 - (a -1)y -a\u00b2 \u22640, for all y \u2208 [-1,1].\n\nWe need to find all negative a such that this holds for all y in [-1,1].\n\nLet me consider the quadratic function in y: Q(y) = y\u00b2 - (a -1)y -a\u00b2.\n\nWe need Q(y) \u22640 for all y \u2208 [-1,1].\n\nFor this to be true, the maximum of Q(y) on [-1,1] must be \u22640.\n\nAs before, since the coefficient of y\u00b2 is positive (1), the parabola opens upwards. Therefore, the maximum occurs at one of the endpoints y=-1 or y=1.\n\nTherefore, compute Q(1) and Q(-1):\n\nQ(1) = 1 - (a -1) -a\u00b2 = 1 -a +1 -a\u00b2 = 2 -a -a\u00b2.\n\nQ(-1) = 1 + (a -1) -a\u00b2 = 1 +a -1 -a\u00b2 = a -a\u00b2.\n\nThus, the maximum of Q(y) on [-1,1] is the maximum of Q(1) and Q(-1).\n\nTherefore, we need both Q(1) \u22640 and Q(-1) \u22640.\n\nWait, hold on! If the maximum of Q(y) is the maximum between Q(1) and Q(-1), then both of these must be \u22640. Therefore, to ensure Q(y) \u22640 for all y \u2208 [-1,1], it's necessary and sufficient that both Q(1) \u22640 and Q(-1) \u22640.\n\nWait, this contradicts our earlier reasoning where we thought only Q(1) needed to be \u22640. Let me check.\n\nSuppose a=-3:\n\nQ(1) =2 -(-3) -(-3)^2=2 +3 -9= -4 \u22640.\n\nQ(-1)= (-3) - (-3)^2= -3 -9= -12 \u22640.\n\nSo both are \u22640. For a=-2:\n\nQ(1)=2 -(-2) -4=2 +2 -4=0 \u22640.\n\nQ(-1)= (-2) -4= -6 \u22640.\n\nFor a=-1.5:\n\nQ(1)=2 -(-1.5) -2.25=2 +1.5 -2.25=1.25>0.\n\nQ(-1)= (-1.5) -2.25= -3.75 \u22640.\n\nSince Q(1) >0, the inequality fails.\n\nFor a=-1:\n\nQ(1)=2 -(-1) -1=2 +1 -1=2>0.\n\nQ(-1)= (-1) -1= -2 \u22640.\n\nAgain, Q(1) >0.\n\nTherefore, to have Q(y) \u22640 for all y \u2208 [-1,1], both Q(1) \u22640 and Q(-1) \u22640 must hold.\n\nTherefore, we need to solve the system:\n\n2 -a -a\u00b2 \u22640,\n\na -a\u00b2 \u22640.\n\nLet's solve these inequalities.\n\nFirst inequality: 2 -a -a\u00b2 \u22640\n\nMultiply by -1: a\u00b2 +a -2 \u22650\n\nAs before, roots at a=(-1 \u00b1\u221a(1 +8))/2=(-1 \u00b13)/2 \u2192 a=1 and 