urs at one of the endpoints \\( y = -1 \\) or \\( y = 1 \\).\n\nEvaluating the quadratic at the endpoints:\n\n1. At \\( y = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   f(1) = 1 - (a - 1) - a^2 = 2 - a - a^2\n   \\]\n\n2. At \\( y = -1 \\):\n   \\[\n   f(-1) = 1 + (a - 1) - a^2 = a - a^2\n   \\]\n\nFor the inequality to hold, both \\( f(1) \\leq 0 \\) and \\( f(-1) \\leq 0 \\) must be satisfied. \n\nFor \\( f(-1) \\leq 0 \\):\n\\[\na - a^2 \\leq 0 \\implies a(1 - a) \\leq 0\n\\]\nSince \\( a \\) is negative, this inequality holds for all \\( a \\leq 0 \\).\n\nFor \\( f(1) \\leq 0 \\):\n\\[\n2 - a - a^2 \\leq 0 \\implies a^2 + a - 2 \\geq 0\n\\]\nSolving the quadratic inequality \\( a^2 + a - 2 \\geq 0 \\), we find the roots \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( a = -2 \\). The inequality holds for \\( a \\leq -2 \\) or \\( a \\geq 1 \\). Since we are considering negative \\( a \\), the valid solution is \\( a \\leq -2 \\).\n\nThus, the range of negative values for \\( a \\) such that the inequality holds for all \\( x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\) is \\(\\boxed{(-\\infty, -2]}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe start with the given inequality:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 x + a \\cos x + a^2 \\geqslant 1 + \\cos x\n\\]\n\n1. **Express \\(\\sin^2 x\\) in terms of \\(\\cos x\\)**:\n   Since \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\), we can rewrite the inequality as:\n   \\[\n   1 - \\cos^2 x + a \\cos x + a^2 \\geqslant 1 + \\cos x\n   \\]\n\n2. **Simplify the inequality**:\n   Subtract 1 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   -\\cos^2 x + a \\cos x + a^2 \\geqslant \\cos x\n   \\]\n   Subtract \\(\\cos x\\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   -\\cos^2 x + (a - 1) \\cos x + a^2 \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n\n3. **Complete the square**:\n   To make this more manageable, we complete the square on the left-hand side. We seek to write it in the form \\((\\cos x - c)^2 + d\\). Specifically:\n   \\[\n   -\\cos^2 x + (a - 1) \\cos x + a^2 = -\\left( \\cos^2 x - (a - 1) \\cos x \\right) + a^2\n   \\]\n   Completing the square inside the parentheses:\n   \\[\n   \\cos^2 x 