ve.\n\nWow, that worked out! So by placing the triangle in coordinate system, computing coordinates of E and F, then calculating the distance EF, after expanding, it simplified to a cos A. That's neat. Let me recap to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\n1. Placed A at (0,0), B at (c,0), C at (b cos A, b sin A). Correct.\n\n2. Found F as projection of C onto AB: (b cos A, 0). Correct, since AB is along x-axis.\n\n3. Found E by projecting B onto AC. Used parametric equations, found parameter t = (c cos A)/b. Then coordinates of E as (c cos\u00b2 A, c cos A sin A). That seems correct.\n\n4. Calculated EF distance. Difference in x: c cos\u00b2 A - b cos A = cos A (c cos A - b). Difference in y: c cos A sin A. Squared and added, factor out cos\u00b2 A, then inside sqrt becomes a\u00b2, so EF = a cos A. Perfect.\n\nYes, that seems correct. Alternatively, is there another way to see this without coordinates?\n\nPerhaps using the projection. Since EF is the distance between the feet of the two altitudes. Let me think. In triangle ABC, the feet E and F are projections of B and C onto AC and AB respectively. Maybe using the formula for the distance between two projections.\n\nAlternatively, consider the quadrilateral BECF. Since BE and CF are altitudes, they intersect at the orthocenter. But not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, using the formula for the length of EF in terms of the sides and angles. Wait, since EF is a segment connecting the feet of the altitudes, maybe using the cosine law in triangle AEF or something.\n\nAlternatively, observe that in triangle ABC, the points E and F lie on the sides AC and AB. Then, the length EF can be related using the cosine of angle A.\n\nWait, in the coordinate system approach, we found that EF = a cos A. The key was expressing coordinates in terms of sides and angle, then computing the distance. The critical step was recognizing that the expression under the square root simplifies to a\u00b2 via the Law of Cosines.\n\nAnother 