art by recalling that if a polynomial is a perfect square, then it can be expressed as the square of another polynomial. Since the given polynomial is a quartic (degree 4), the polynomial we're squaring must be a quadratic (degree 2). So, let me denote that quadratic as \\( P(x) = x^{2} + px + q \\). Then, when we square this, we should get the original polynomial. Let me write that out:\n\n\\( P(x)^2 = (x^{2} + px + q)^2 \\)\n\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\n\\( x^{4} + 2px^{3} + (p^{2} + 2q)x^{2} + 2pqx + q^{2} \\)\n\nNow, we need this to equal the given polynomial \\( x^{4} + x^{3} + 2x^{2} + ax + b \\). Therefore, the coefficients of corresponding terms must be equal. Let me set up equations by matching the coefficients:\n\n1. Coefficient of \\( x^{4} \\): 1 (on both sides, so that's already matched)\n2. Coefficient of \\( x^{3} \\): \\( 2p = 1 \\)\n3. Coefficient of \\( x^{2} \\): \\( p^{2} + 2q = 2 \\)\n4. Coefficient of \\( x \\): \\( 2pq = a \\)\n5. Constant term: \\( q^{2} = b \\)\n\nOkay, so now I can solve these equations step by step. Let's start with the coefficient of \\( x^{3} \\):\n\nFrom equation 2: \\( 2p = 1 \\) implies \\( p = \\frac{1}{2} \\)\n\nThen, substitute \\( p = \\frac{1}{2} \\) into equation 3:\n\n\\( (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} + 2q = 2 \\)\n\nCalculating \\( (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} = \\frac{1}{4} \\), so:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{4} + 2q = 2 \\)\n\nSubtract \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\) from both sides:\n\n\\( 2q = 2 - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{8}{4} - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{7}{4} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( q = \\frac{7}{8} \\)\n\nNow, moving to equation 4: \\( 2pq = a \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( p = \\frac{1}{2} \\) and \\( q = \\frac{7}{8} \\):\n\n\\( 2 * \\frac{1}{2} * \\frac{7}{8} = a \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( \\frac{7}{8} = a \\)\n\nSo, \\( a = \\frac{7}{8} \\)\n\nThen, equation 5: \\( q^{2} = b \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( q = \\frac{7}{8} \\):\n\n\\( (\\frac{7}{8})^{2} = \\frac{49}{64} = b \\)\n\nThus, \\( b = \\frac{49}{64} \\)\n\nWait a minute, let me verify this because when I