m confident in the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe values of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{7}{8}}\\) and \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{49}{64}}\\), respectively.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the values of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) such that the polynomial \\( x^4 + x^3 + 2x^2 + ax + b \\) is a perfect square, we start by expressing it as the square of a quadratic polynomial \\( P(x) = x^2 + px + q \\). Expanding \\( P(x)^2 \\) gives:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + px + q)^2 = x^4 + 2px^3 + (p^2 + 2q)x^2 + 2pqx + q^2\n\\]\n\nWe compare the coefficients of this expanded form with the given polynomial \\( x^4 + x^3 + 2x^2 + ax + b \\):\n\n1. Coefficient of \\( x^3 \\): \\( 2p = 1 \\) implies \\( p = \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n2. Coefficient of \\( x^2 \\): \\( p^2 + 2q = 2 \\). Substituting \\( p = \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 + 2q = 2 \\implies \\frac{1}{4} + 2q = 2 \\implies 2q = \\frac{7}{4} \\implies q = \\frac{7}{8}\n   \\]\n3. Coefficient of \\( x \\): \\( 2pq = a \\). Substituting \\( p = \\frac{1}{2} \\) and \\( q = \\frac{7}{8} \\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right) = \\frac{7}{8} \\implies a = \\frac{7}{8}\n   \\]\n4. Constant term: \\( q^2 = b \\). Substituting \\( q = \\frac{7}{8} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)^2 = \\frac{49}{64} \\implies b = \\frac{49}{64}\n   \\]\n\nVerification by expanding \\( \\left(x^2 + \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)^2 \\) confirms the result matches the given polynomial. Therefore, the values of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{7}{8}} \\text{ and } \\boxed{\\dfrac{49}{64}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "To determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) for the expression \\(x^4 + x^3 + 2x^2 + ax + b\\) to be a perfect square, we assume it can be written as a square of a quadratic trinomial. Therefore, assume:\n\\[\nx^4 + x^3 + 2x^2 + ax + b = \\left( x^2 + px + q \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand the right-hand side and compare the coefficients with the left-hand side. First, expand the square:\n\\[\n\\left( x^2 + px + q \\right)^2 = x^4 + 2px^3 + (p^2 + 2q)x^2 + 2pqx + q^2\n\\]\n\nWe now equate the coefficients of respective powers of \\(x\\):\n\n1. Comparing the coefficients of \\(x^3\\):\n   \\[\n   2p = 1 \\implies p = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   \n2. Comparing the coefficients of \\(x^2\\):\n   \\[\n   p^2 + 2q = 2 \\implies \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 + 2q = 2 \\implies \\frac{1}{4} + 2q = 2 \\implies 2q = 2 - \\frac{1}{4} \\implies 2q = \\frac{7}{4} \\implies q = \\frac{7}{8}\n   \\]\n   \n3. Comparing the coefficients of \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   2pq = a \\implies 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\left( \\frac{7}{8} \\right) = a \\implies \\frac{7}{8} = a \\implies a = \\frac{7}{8}\n   \\]\n\n4. Comparing the coefficients of the constant term:\n   \\[\n   q^2 = b \\implies \\left( \\frac{7}{8} \\right)^2 = b \\implies b = \\frac{49}{64}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that make the polynomial \\(x^4 + x^3 + 2x^2 + ax + b\\) a perfect square are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{a = \\frac{7}{8}, \\quad b = \\frac{49}{64}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There was a 10-on-10 football match between a team of liars (who always lie) and a team of truth-tellers (who always tell the truth). After the match, each player was asked: \"How many goals did you score?\" Some participants answered \"one\", Misha said \"two\", some answered \"three\", and the rest said \"five\". Is Misha lying if the truth-tellers won with a score of $20:17$?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem. So, there's a football match between two teams: one team is all liars, and the other is all truth-tellers. It was 10 players on each side, so 10 liars and 10 truth-tellers. After the match, each player was asked how many goals they scored. Some said \"one\", Misha said \"two\", some said \"three\", and the rest said \"five\". The truth-tellers won with a score of 20:17. The question is: Is Misha lying?\n\nFirst, let's parse the information. The truth-tellers won 20-17. So, the truth-tellers scored 20 goals, and the liars scored 17. Since the teams are composed of players who either always lie or always tell the truth, their answers about how many goals they scored must be either false or true, respectively.\n\nEach player was asked, so all 20 players (10 from each team) answered. The possible answers were \"one\", \"two\" (only Misha), \"three\", and \"five\". So, the answers are distributed as follows: some number of players answered \"one\", one player (Misha) answered \"two\", some answered \"three\", and the rest answered \"five\".\n\nFirst, we need to determine which team Misha is on. If Misha is a truth-teller, then he actually scored two goals. If he's a liar, then he didn't score two goals. Since the final score is 20:17, and the truth-tellers won, their total goals must be 20, and the liars' total is 17. So, we need to figure out the distribution of goals based on the players' answers and their team (liar or truth-teller).\n\nLet me break this down step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the teams' composition:**\n   - Truth-tellers: 10 players. Each of their answers is true. So, if a truth-teller answered \"one\", they scored one goal; if \"three\", they scored three goals, etc.\n   - Liars: 10 players. Each of their answers is false. So, if a liar answered \"one\", they didn't score one goal; if they answered \"two\", they didn't score two, etc. Since Misha is one of the players, we need to figure out which team he is on.\n\n2. **Determine possible goal contributions:**\n   - For truth-tellers: Their stated goals are accurate. So, the sum of their answers should equal their total score (20).\n   - For liars: Their stated goals are false. So, each liar's actual goals are different from what they claimed. The sum of their actual goals should equal 17.\n\nBut wait, the problem stat