a) who scored 2, plus other truth-tellers who answered \"one\", \"three\", or \"five\". The total goals from truth-tellers would be:\n\nTotal truth-tellers' goals = T1*1 + 2 (from Misha) + T3*3 + T5*5 = 20.\n\nAlso, the number of truth-tellers is 10. So:\n\nT1 + 1 (Misha) + T3 + T5 = 10.\n\nSimilarly, the liars: their answers must be false. The liars would have answered \"one\", \"three\", or \"five\" (since Misha is the only \"two\"). Let L1, L3, L5 be the number of liars who answered \"one\", \"three\", \"five\" respectively. So:\n\nL1 + L3 + L5 = 10 (since there are 10 liars).\n\nBut the total goals from liars must be 17. Since liars cannot have scored the number they answered, each liar who answered \"one\" scored something not 1, each who answered \"three\" scored not 3, etc. So:\n\nTotal liars' goals = sum over each liar's actual goals.\n\nThis complicates things because we don't know how much each liar actually scored. But we need to find integer values that satisfy these conditions.\n\nHowever, this seems complex. Maybe there's another approach.\n\nAlternatively, since the truth-tellers' total is 20, which must be the sum of their answers. Let's denote that the truth-tellers' answers are some number of \"ones\", \"threes\", \"fives\", and Misha's \"two\". So:\n\nTotal truth-tellers' goals = (Number of truth-tellers who said \"one\")*1 + 1*2 (Misha) + (Number of truth-tellers who said \"three\")*3 + (Number of truth-tellers who said \"five\")*5 = 20.\n\nAlso, the number of truth-tellers is 10, so:\n\n(Number of truth-tellers who said \"one\") + 1 (Misha) + (Number of truth-tellers who said \"three\") + (Number of truth-tellers who said \"five\") = 10.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nLet a = number of truth-tellers who said \"one\".\n\nLet b = number of truth-tellers who said \"three\".\n\nLet c = number of truth-tellers who said \"five\".\n\nThen:\n\na + b + c + 1 = 10 \u21d2 a + b + c = 9.\n\nAnd:\n\n1*a + 3*b + 5*c + 2 = 20 \u21d2 a + 3b + 5c = 18.\n\nSo we have two equations:\n\n1. a + b + c = 9\n\n2. a + 3b + 5c = 18\n\nSubtracting equation 1 from equation 2:\n\n(3b + 5c) - (b + c) = 18 - 9 \u21d2 2b + 4c = 9 \u21d2 b + 2c = 4.5.\n\nBut b and c are integers, so this equation cannot hold. Because the left side is an integer and the right side is 4.5. Therefore, this is impossible. Hence, there is no solution if Misha is a truth-teller. Therefore, Misha must be a liar.\n\nWait, that's a key point. If we assume Misha is a truth-teller, we end up with an impossible equation (b + 2c = 4.5), which can't be true since b and c must be integers. Therefore, Misha cannot be a truth-teller. Hence, he must be a liar. Therefore, Misha is lying when he said \"two\".\n\nBut wait, let's verify this conclusion carefully.\n\n**Case 1: Misha is a truth-teller**\n\nAs above, leads to a contradiction because 2b + 4c = 9 \u21d2 impossible. Hence, Misha can't be a truth-teller. Therefore, he's a liar. Therefore, his statement is false, so he didn't score two goals. Hence, the answer is yes, Misha is lying.\n\nBut let's check the other case to be thorough.\n\n**Case 2: Misha is a liar.**\n\nSo, Misha is on the liar team. Therefore, the truth-tellers consist of 10 players, none of whom answered \"two\". The liars consist of 9 other players plus Misha. The truth-tellers' answers are \"one\", \"three\", \"five\", and their goals are equal to their answers. The liars' answers are \"one\", \"three\", \"five\", and Misha's \"two\", but their actual goals are different from their answers.\n\nSo, for the truth-tellers:\n\nLet a = number of truth-tellers who answered \"one\".\n\nb = number of truth-tellers who answered \"three\".\n\nc = number of truth-tellers who answered \"five\".\n\nThen:\n\na + b + c = 10 (since all 10 truth-tellers answered either \"one\", \"three\", or \"five\").\n\nTheir total goals: a*1 + b*3 + c*5 = 20.\n\nFor the liars:\n\nThey have 10 players: Misha (who answered \"two\") and 9 others who answered \"one\", \"three\", or \"five\"