ondition. Hence, quadratic polynomials cannot satisfy the condition. \n\nTherefore, the only solutions are linear functions with rational coefficients and any real constants. \n\nThus, the answer is all polynomials f(x) of the form f(x) = qx + c where q is a rational number and c is a real number.", "deepseek_solution": "The polynomials \\( f(x) \\) of degree no higher than two that satisfy the condition are precisely those of the form:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = qx + c\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( q \\) is a **rational number** and \\( c \\) is any **real number**. This includes constant functions (when \\( q = 0 \\)).\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **General Form:** Assume \\( f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\), where \\( a, b, c \\in \\mathbb{R} \\) and \\( \\deg(f) \\leq 2 \\).\n\n2. **Difference Expression:** Compute \\( f(x) - f(y) \\):\n   \\[\n   f(x) - f(y) = a(x^2 - y^2) + b(x - y) = (x - y)\\left(a(x + y) + b\\right).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Condition Analysis:** For \\( x - y \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\), the product \\( (x - y)\\left(a(x + y) + b\\right) \\) must be rational. Let \\( r = x - y \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\). Substitute \\( x = y + r \\):\n   \\[\n   a(x + y) + b = a(2y + r) + b = 2a y + (a r + b).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Rationality Constraint:** For \\( 2a y + (a r + b) \\) to be rational **for all real \\( y \\)** (since \\( y \\) can be arbitrary as long as \\( x = y + r \\) is real):\n   - The term \\( 2a y \\) must vanish for irrational \\( y \\), forcing \\( a = 0 \\). Otherwise, \\( 2a y \\) could be irrational even if \\( a \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\n5. **Linear Case:** If \\( a = 0 \\), then \\( f(x) = bx + c \\). The difference becomes:\n   \\[\n   f(x) - f(y) = b(x - y).\n   \\]\n   For this to be rational whenever \\( x - y \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\), \\( b \\) must be rational. The constant \\( c \\) cancels out and can be any real number.\n\n6. **Quadratic Exclusion:** If \\( a \\neq 0 \\), even with \\( a \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\), choosing \\( y \\) irrational makes \\( 2a y + (a r + b) \\) 