 rational number.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nHence, the polynomials \\( f(x) \\) that satisfy the given condition are linear functions with rational coefficients or constant functions.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(x) = bx + c \\text{ with } b \\text{ rational and } c \\text{ any real number}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Three cells in a grid contain numbers, and all other cells are empty. It is allowed to choose two numbers from different non-empty cells and write their sum in an empty cell. It is also allowed to choose numbers \\(a, b, c\\) from three different non-empty cells and write the number \\(ab + c^2\\) in an empty cell. Prove that using a series of such operations, it is possible to write in one of the cells the square of the sum of the three initial numbers, no matter what these numbers are.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that starting with three numbers in a grid, using these two operations\u2014either adding two numbers from different cells and putting the sum in an empty cell, or taking three numbers from different cells and computing ab + c\u00b2 and putting that in an empty cell\u2014I can eventually get the square of the sum of the three initial numbers in one of the cells. Hmm, let me try to break this down.\n\nFirst, let me note down the operations clearly:\n\n1. **Sum Operation**: Choose two numbers from different non-empty cells and write their sum in an empty cell.\n2. **Product-Quadratic Operation**: Choose three numbers \\(a, b, c\\) from three different non-empty cells and write \\(ab + c^2\\) in an empty cell.\n\nThe goal is to create \\((x + y + z)^2\\) where \\(x, y, z\\) are the initial numbers in the three cells. Let's call these numbers \\(a_1, a_2, a_3\\) for clarity. So we need to create \\((a_1 + a_2 + a_3)^2\\). Expanding this, it's \\(a_1^2 + a_2^2 + a_3^2 + 2a_1a_2 + 2a_1a_3 + 2a_2a_3\\). So the challenge is to generate all these terms using the giv