{2}, \\frac{1 - a - b}{2}\\right)\\)\n\nWhich means both \\(\\frac{1 + a - b}{2} > 0\\) and \\(\\frac{1 - a - b}{2} > 0\\). Therefore:\n\n1. \\(1 + a - b > 0 \\implies a - b > -1\\)\n2. \\(1 - a - b > 0 \\implies -a - b > -1 \\implies a + b < 1\\)\n\nBut since \\(b \\geq 0\\), and \\(a\\) can be positive or negative. However, the original condition is \\(|a| + |b| < 1\\). Let's see how this relates.\n\nWait, if \\(b \\geq 0\\), then \\(|b| = b\\), so \\(|a| + b < 1\\). The two inequalities from above are:\n\n1. \\(a - b > -1\\)\n2. \\(a + b < 1\\)\n\nBut these can be rewritten as:\n\n1. \\(a > b - 1\\)\n2. \\(a < 1 - b\\)\n\nBut if \\(|a| + b < 1\\), then:\n\n- If \\(a \\geq 0\\), \\(|a| = a, so a + b < 1\\)\n- If \\(a < 0\\), \\(|a| = -a, so -a + b < 1 \\implies b - a < 1 \\implies a > b - 1\\)\n\nSo combining these, when \\(b \\geq 0\\), \\(|a| + b < 1\\) is equivalent to \\( - (1 - b) < a < 1 - b \\), which is exactly the two inequalities above. Therefore, the existence of \\(x_4\\) is equivalent to \\(|a| + b < 1\\). But since in this case \\(b \\geq 0\\), \\(|a| + |b| = |a| + b < 1\\).\n\nCase 2: \\(b < 0\\)\n\nThen, \\(\\max(0, -b) = -b = |b|\\), so the inequality becomes:\n\n\\( |b| < \\min\\left(\\frac{1 + a - b}{2}, \\frac{1 - a - b}{2}\\right) \\)\n\nWhich requires both:\n\n1. \\( |b| < \\frac{1 + a - b}{2} \\)\n2. \\( |b| < \\frac{1 - a - b}{2} \\)\n\nLet me analyze each inequality.\n\nFirst inequality:\n\n\\( |b| < \\frac{1 + a - b}{2} \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n\\( 2|b| < 1 + a - b \\)\n\nBut since \\(b < 0\\), \\(|b| = -b\\), so:\n\n\\( -2b < 1 + a - b \\implies -2b + b < 1 + a \\implies -b < 1 + a \\implies a + b > -1 \\)\n\nSecond inequality:\n\n\\( |b| < \\frac{1 - a - b}{2} \\)\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n\\( 2|b| < 1 - a - b \\)\n\nAgain, \\(b < 0\\), so \\(|b| = -b\\):\n\n\\( -2b < 1 - a - b \\implies -2b + b < 1 - a \\implies -b < 1 - a \\implies a - b < 1 \\implies a < 1 + b \\)\n\nBut since \\(b < 0\\), \\(1 + b < 1\\).\n\nWait, let's check: