934 all pass through P, they become lines L1, L2, L3, L4 respectively.\n\nMoreover, since \u03931 and \u03933 were tangent at P, their images L1 and L3 under inversion must be parallel lines. Because two circles tangent at the center of inversion invert to two parallel lines (since the point of tangency is at P, which becomes the \"point at infinity\" for the lines, so parallel lines don't meet). Similarly, \u03932 and \u03934 tangent at P invert to parallel lines L2 and L4.\n\nWait, actually, when you invert two circles tangent at P, which is the center of inversion, their images will be lines that don't meet because the original circles were tangent only at P, which is the center. But lines inverted from circles tangent at the center would actually be parallel lines. Yes, because the tangent point becomes the point at infinity, so their images are lines whose \"meeting point\" is at infinity, hence parallel.\n\nSo, after inversion, we have four lines: L1 and L3 are parallel, L2 and L4 are parallel. The other intersections of the original circles (A, B, C, D) will invert to points A', B', C', D' which lie on the inverted lines.\n\nLet me detail this:\n\n- \u03931 and \u03932 intersect at A and P. After inversion, L1 and L2 intersect at A' (image of A) and at the image of P, which is the point at infinity. But since lines L1 and L2 are not parallel (unless \u03931 and \u03932 were tangent, which they are not, since they intersect at two points A and P). Wait, but \u03931 and \u03932 are not tangent, they meet at A and P. So after inversion, L1 and L2 are two lines intersecting at A' (since P inverts to infinity, so the intersection at P becomes an intersection at infinity, which for two lines would mean they are parallel if they don't meet elsewhere. Wait, no. Wait, if two circles intersect at P and another point A, then after inversion, the images are two lines that intersect only at A', since P is inverted to infinity. Therefore, two lines that intersect at A' and