? Because (a\u2099 - 1)^2 = a\u2099\u00b2 - 2a\u2099 + 1, so adding 1 gives a\u2099\u00b2 - 2a\u2099 + 2. So sqrt(a\u2099\u00b2 - 2a\u2099 + 2) = sqrt((a\u2099 - 1)^2 + 1). Hmm, but that might not immediately help. Wait, but maybe there's a substitution that can simplify the recursion.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this sequence is related to trigonometric functions or hyperbolic functions, where such expressions appear. Alternatively, maybe if we consider substitution to simplify the recurrence. Let me try to let b\u2099 = a\u2099 - 1. Then maybe the recursion becomes easier. Let's try that.\n\nLet me define b\u2099 = a\u2099 - 1. Then a\u2099 = b\u2099 + 1. Then the recursion becomes:\n\na\u2099\u208a\u2081 = sqrt((b\u2099 + 1)^2 - 2(b\u2099 + 1) + 2) + 1.\n\nLet me compute the expression inside the square root:\n\n(b\u2099 + 1)^2 - 2(b\u2099 + 1) + 2 = b\u2099\u00b2 + 2b\u2099 + 1 - 2b\u2099 - 2 + 2 = b\u2099\u00b2 + (2b\u2099 - 2b\u2099) + (1 - 2 + 2) = b\u2099\u00b2 + 1.\n\nSo the recursion simplifies to a\u2099\u208a\u2081 = sqrt(b\u2099\u00b2 + 1) + 1. But since a\u2099\u208a\u2081 = b\u2099\u208a\u2081 + 1, substituting gives:\n\nb\u2099\u208a\u2081 + 1 = sqrt(b\u2099\u00b2 + 1) + 1. Subtract 1 from both sides: b\u2099\u208a\u2081 = sqrt(b\u2099\u00b2 + 1).\n\nWait, that seems recursive. If b\u2099\u208a\u2081 = sqrt(b\u2099\u00b2 + 1), that suggests each term is the square root of the previous term squared plus 1. But that seems like a hyperbolic function. Let me test with the initial term. Since a\u2081 = 1, then b\u2081 = a\u2081 - 1 = 0. So b\u2081 = 0.\n\nThen b\u2082 = sqrt(b\u2081\u00b2 + 1) = sqrt(0 + 1) = 1.\n\nThen b\u2083 = sqrt(b\u2082\u00b2 + 1) = sqrt(1 + 1) = sqrt(2).\n\nSimilarly, b\u2084 = sqrt( (sqrt(2))\u00b2 + 1 ) = sqrt(2 + 1) = sqrt(3).\n\nWait, so in general, b\u2099 = sqrt(n - 1). Wait, check:\n\nb\u2081 = 0 = sqrt(1 - 1) = sqrt(0) = 0. Correct.\n\nb\u2082 = 1 = sqrt(2 - 1) = sqrt(1) = 1. Correct.\n\nb\u2083 = sqrt(2) = sqrt(3 - 1) = sqrt