et me check again.\n\nWait, the volume is (1/3)\u03c0h\u00b2 * side_length. But h is the height corresponding to that side, so h = 2A / side_length. Therefore, substituting h into the volume formula:\n\nVolume = (1/3)\u03c0*(2A / side_length)\u00b2 * side_length = (1/3)\u03c0*(4A\u00b2 / side_length\u00b2) * side_length = (4/3)\u03c0A\u00b2 / side_length.\n\nTherefore, Volume = (4/3)\u03c0A\u00b2 / side_length.\n\nTherefore, the volume is inversely proportional to the side length. So, if we want to maximize the volume, we need to minimize the denominator, i.e., choose the smallest side.\n\nHence, the maximum volume is achieved when rotating around the shortest side.\n\nBut wait, let's verify this with an example. Suppose we have a triangle with sides 3, 4, 5. It's a right-angled triangle. Let's compute the volumes when rotating around each side.\n\nFirst, sides: 3, 4, 5. Semi-perimeter s = (3+4+5)/2 = 6. Area A = sqrt[6(6-3)(6-4)(6-5)] = sqrt[6*3*2*1] = sqrt[36] = 6.\n\nHeights:\n\nh_3 = 2A / 3 = 12 / 3 = 4\n\nh_4 = 2A / 4 = 12 / 4 = 3\n\nh_5 = 2A / 5 = 12 / 5 = 2.4\n\nVolumes:\n\nAround side 3: (1/3)\u03c0*(4)^2*3 = (1/3)\u03c0*16*3 = 16\u03c0\n\nAround side 4: (1/3)\u03c0*(3)^2*4 = (1/3)\u03c0*9*4 = 12\u03c0\n\nAround side 5: (1/3)\u03c0*(2.4)^2*5 \u2248 (1/3)\u03c0*5.76*5 \u2248 9.6\u03c0\n\nSo indeed, the volumes are 16\u03c0, 12\u03c0, and ~9.6\u03c0. The largest is 16\u03c0 when rotating around the side of length 3, which is the shortest side. So in this case, rotating around the shortest side gives the maximum volume.\n\nAnother example: take an equilateral triangle with sides of length 2 each. The area A = (sqrt(3)/4)*2\u00b2 = sqrt(3). Heights h = (2A)/2 = sqrt(3). So rotating around any side would give the same volume, since all sides are equal. So in this case, all volumes are equal, which makes sense.\n\nAnother example: a triangle with sides 5, 5, 6. Semi-perimeter s = (5+5+6)/2 = 8. Area A = sqrt[8*(8-5)*(8-5)*(8-6)] = sqrt[8*3*3*2] = sqrt[144] = 12.\n