ny different P's can lie on various circles around Q?\n\nBut unless restricted, there could be up to n-1 such P's (all other points). But perhaps there's a way to limit the number of times Q can be on other circles.\n\nAlternatively, consider that for each point Q, the number of circles (from other centers) that contain Q is equal to the number of distinct distances from Q to other points. Because each circle around some P that contains Q corresponds to the distance PQ. So if Q has t distinct distances to other points, then Q is on t circles (one for each distance, with centers at the points at that distance). But in our problem, each point Q is on at least k circles (since each P has a circle containing at least k points, but unless Q is one of those k points for many P's). Wait, no. Each P has a circle containing at least k points, but which points those are can vary. So Q might be on many circles or few.\n\nBut the problem doesn't specify that each point is on many circles, only that each center has many points on its circle. So perhaps the average number of circles that a point is on is k. So total incidences n*k.\n\nBut going back, since each pair (P, Q) can be counted at most twice, once from P's circle and once from Q's circle, if both include each other. But maybe if we can show that the number of incidences is bounded by something like 2n*sqrt(n), then n*k <= 2n*sqrt(n) => k <= 2*sqrt(n). But the problem wants sqrt(2n), so maybe with a better coefficient.\n\nAlternatively, use graph theory. If we model the points as a graph where each point has degree at least k, then the graph has at least n*k/2 edges. But in reality, it's a directed graph with n*k edges, but perhaps we can relate this to an undirected graph. However, in the undirected graph, each edge is present if there's a circle in one direction or both. But since edges can be one-directional, the undirected graph might have fewer edges. But maybe Turan's theorem can be applied here, but Turan's theore