ber of matches is 1. That's pretty neat.\n\nNow, for the variance. The variance of a sum of random variables isn't as straightforward because it involves covariance terms. The formula for variance of a sum is the sum of variances plus twice the sum of covariances for each pair. So, if we let X_i be the indicator variable for a match in position i, then the total number of matches is X = X\u2081 + X\u2082 + ... + X_n. Then, Var(X) = Var(X\u2081 + X\u2082 + ... + X_n) = \u03a3 Var(X_i) + 2\u03a3\u03a3 Cov(X_i, X_j) for i < j.\n\nFirst, let's compute Var(X_i). Since each X_i is a Bernoulli trial with p = 1/n, the variance is p(1 - p) = (1/n)(1 - 1/n) = (n - 1)/n\u00b2.\n\nNow, there are n terms in the sum of variances, so that part would be n*(n - 1)/n\u00b2 = (n - 1)/n.\n\nThen, the covariance terms. For each pair i \u2260 j, Cov(X_i, X_j) = E[X_i X_j] - E[X_i]E[X_j]. Since X_i and X_j are indicators, E[X_i X_j] is the probability that both position i and position j are matches. E[X_i]E[X_j] is (1/n)(1/n) = 1/n\u00b2. So, we need to compute E[X_i X_j], which is the probability that both positions i and j are matches.\n\nTo compute this probability, we need to consider the permutation of the second deck relative to the first. If the first deck is in fixed order, the second deck is a permutation of the first. The number of permutations where both position i and position j are fixed (i.e., derangements with two fixed points) is (n - 2)! So the probability is (n - 2)! / n! = 1 / [n(n - 1)]. Therefore, E[X_i X_j] = 1 / [n(n - 1)].\n\nThus, Cov(X_i, X_j) = 1/[n(n - 1)] - 1/n\u00b2 = [n - (n - 1)] / [n\u00b2(n - 1)] = 1 / [n\u00b2(n - 1)] - Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, E[X_i X_j] = 1/[n(n - 1)], and E[X_i]E[X_j] = 1/n\u00b2. So Cov(X_i, X_j) = 1/[n(n - 1)] - 1/n\u00b2 = [n - (n - 1)] / [n\u00b2(n - 1)] = 1 / [n\u00b2(n - 1)].\n\nWait, let me compute that again:\n\n1/[n(n - 1)] - 1/n\u00b2 = (1/n(n - 1)) - (1/n\u00b2) = [n - (n - 1)] / [n\u00b2(n - 1)] = [1] / [n\u